#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096358 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d707bc81-53a8-55c5-a545-e9ccdeedaa86" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area Bounded by a Curve, the y-Axis, and Two Horizontal Lines"}] :question/id #uuid "2ca27070-9f89-5679-98f9-5ac22b1c3f6d" :question/math-academy-id "e-425" :question/problem "Find the area bounded between the curve $x=y^2-2y+2$, the $y$-axis, and the lines $y=1$ and $y=2$, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1041/Source/Images/ba7fd1150e24e1bce909556ef88947a8.png)" :question/worked-solution "To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= ∫_{c}^{d}x(y)dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{1}^{2}(y^{2} - 2y + 2)dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{1}^{2}y^{2}dy - 2∫_{1}^{2}ydy + ∫_{1}^{2}2dy \\\\\n&= \\frac{y^{3}}{3}\\begin{vmatrix}_{1}^{2} - 2 \\cdot \\frac{y^{2}}{2} & _{1}^{2} + 2y\\end{vmatrix}_{1}^{2} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{8}{3} - \\frac{1}{3}) - (4 - 1) + (4 - 2) \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{3} - 3 + 2 \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096359 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "384ce5b6-40da-59a0-bc87-a1d726328cc2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Finite Area Bounded Between a Curve and the Y-Axis"}] :question/id #uuid "74b5f2a7-5526-5711-b269-6d390132262e" :question/math-academy-id "e-426" :question/problem "Find the area of the closed region between the curve $x=y^2-4$ and the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the $y$-intercepts of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{2} - 4 &= 0 \\\\\ny^{2} &= 4 \\\\\ny &= ± 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we draw a sketch of the region:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1041/Source/Images/0a73f14099d8a6940e2ee8a6785c25e2.png)\n\nWe want the area between the curve $x=y^2-4$ and the $y$-axis from $y=-2$ to $y=2$. To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= ∫_{c}^{d}x(y)dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{-2}^{2}(y^{2} - 4)dy \\\\\n&= 2∫_{0}^{2}(y^{2} - 4)dy \\\\\n&= 2∫_{0}^{2}y^{2}dy - 8∫_{0}^{2}dy \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot \\frac{y^{3}}{3} \\mid _{0}^{2} - 8y \\mid _{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= 2(\\frac{8}{3} - 0) - 8(2 - 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{16}{3} - 16 \\\\\n&=-\\frac{32}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtained a negative number because the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis. So the area that we want is\n\n$$\n\\left|-\\dfrac{32}{3}\\right| = \\dfrac{32}{3}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096476 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "963f66a5-f742-5b3b-8ae1-f0dbf268bff0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area Bounded Between a Curve and the Y-Axis by First Rewriting the Function"}] :question/id #uuid "12c2517b-f25b-5a54-8072-7ba6390e54ca" :question/math-academy-id "e-6797" :question/problem "Find the area between the curve $y=\\sqrt{x+1}$, the $y$-axis, and the lines $y=0$ and $y=1$, as shown above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1041/Source/Images/8eb4608fe3ade4f8a475b24df1952eb2.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we make $x$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\sqrt{x + 1} \\\\\ny^{2} &= x + 1 \\\\\nx &= y^{2} - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= ∫_{0}^{1}(y^{2} - 1)dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}y^{2}dy - ∫_{0}^{1}dy \\\\\n&= \\frac{y^{3}}{3} \\mid _{0}^{1} - y \\mid _{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{1}{3} - 0) - (1 - 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3} - 1 \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtained a negative number because the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis. So the area that we want is\n\n$$\n\\left|-\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right| = \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$"}]}
